WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
New Zealand's 1080 drops targeted possums to protect forests and cattle, yet stotes turned sharply

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:14.120
on kiwi and other ground birds. This is Unintended Consequences, episode 134. In the steep

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.900
valleys of New Zealand's central north island, helicopters flew low over native bush

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:25.540
in the early 2000s. They released cereal baits laced with sodium fluroacetate. The goal was

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:30.520
straight forward. Managers wanted to reduce the dense populations of brush-tailed possums

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:36.160
that were stripping canopy leaves and carrying tuberculosis to dairy herds. Within a season,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.860
the possum numbers fell sharply. Yet nest cameras placed under the same ridges recorded stotes

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.540
entering Kiwi burrows and taking eggs and chicks at noticeably higher rates than in untreated areas

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.980
nearby. The same landscape that had been chosen for its thick forest cover and remote terrain

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.220
Maine now showed a measurable rise in ground bird losses precisely where the baiting had succeeded

00:00:58.220 --> 00:00:59.140
most completely.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.980
This outcome emerged not from any change in the stots themselves, but from the sudden absence

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.180
of the possums that had previously supplied most of their food.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:14.600
The Arial-1080 program grew from decisions made by New Zealand's agricultural and conservation

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.160
agencies beginning in the 1950s.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.240
Farmers and foresters faced two linked problems.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.800
Possums, introduced from Australia in the 19th century, were defoliating native trees

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.340
at scale.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.560
The same animals were acting as maintenance hosts for bovine tuberculosis that threatened

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.520
the country's export dairy industry.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:39.880
At the time, 1080 offered a practical way to treat rugged terrain that ground teams could

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.980
not reach efficiently.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.780
Officials calculated that lowering possum density would both restore forest health and break

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.580
the tuberculosis cycle in cattle.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.740
The approach aligned with the best available veterinary and ecological understanding then

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.380
current in government departments.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.640
They weighed the cost of repeated ground operations against the single-pass coverage and aircraft

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.720
could achieve across thousands of hectares of steep country.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.840
The arithmetic favored the aerial route because it reduced labor hours while still delivering

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.200
a high percentage kill of the target species.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.280
No one at those planning tables treated the forest as a collection of isolated species.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:23.300
They treated it as a production system whose canopy and whose livestock both needed protection from the same introduced mammal.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:33.340
From the 1950s onward, the pesticides board and later the animal health board authorized regular aerial operations across hundreds of thousands of hectares.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.700
Early results showed clear reductions in possum trap catch indices.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:43.680
In some regions, measurable drops in tuberculosis reactor rates among cattle herds followed.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:50.280
code. Proponents describe the method as cost-effective and selective for the target species. Some field

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:56.440
staff noted that stote numbers sometimes appeared to fluctuate after operations, yet these observations

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:02.880
remained secondary to the primary metrics of possum kill and forest canopy recovery. Operations

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.400
continued on roughly three-year cycles in many blocks through the 1990s and into the 2000s. Each

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.880
which psychically began with pre-feed trials to confirm bait acceptance.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.340
The toxic drop followed, timed for late winter when natural food was scarce.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.060
Post-operation monitoring then counted dead possums and checked residual trap catch rates.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.300
The three-year interval was chosen because it matched the time it took possum populations

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:34.140
to rebound to the density threshold that again threatened both trees and cattle.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:39.140
Managers could schedule the next flight without waiting for visible damage to reappear.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:46.100
When possum numbers collapsed after a 1-080 drop, the main prey base for stoats also shrank.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:52.100
Stoats, themselves introduced in the 1880s to control rabbits, responded by shifting their

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:56.880
diet toward the smaller, more vulnerable ground nesting birds that shared the same habitat.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.080
Nest camera studies later documented elevated stote attack rates inside treated blocks for

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.840
two to three years after each operation. This pattern was consistent with mesopredator release.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:14.140
Kiwi chicks in particular faced increased predation pressure precisely in the areas where

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:20.120
conservation managers had hoped to give them respite. The effect was not uniform across all sites,

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:26.260
yet the direction of change repeated a cost multiple operations. Forest understory recovery

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:32.320
proceeded. Still, the short-term cost fell on the bird species the program had not been designed

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:38.280
to consider. Managers had optimized for one introduced mammal without fully modeling the food

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:43.940
web connections that linked that mammal to another predator and to native prey. Consider the

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:49.880
arithmetic. A single hectare might hold several dozen possums before the drop. After the drop,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.480
that number could fall below one. A stote that had previously taken one possum every few days

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:01.420
now found itself searching for food daily. The nearest alternative prey of comparable size and

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:07.720
accessibility were kiwi eggs and chicks that nested on the forest floor. The 2-3-year window of

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:12.980
elevated pressure matched the time required for stote numbers themselves to decline or for

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:18.800
possum numbers to begin recovering. So the spike in bird losses was temporary but still measurable

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:24.760
in nest success data. One objection sometimes raised is that stotes might simply have moved to

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:30.620
untreated areas. The camera records however showed the increase occurring inside the treated blocks

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:35.700
blocks themselves. This indicated a local diet switch rather than large scale migration.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:41.720
Once the nest camera evidence accumulated, regional pest control plans began to incorporate

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:48.520
simultaneous or follow-up stote trapping in 1080 treated blocks. Some operations shifted

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:53.440
toward integrated timing so that stote numbers could be reduced before the possum crash fully

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.260
altered their foraging. The change added cost and complexity, yet it addressed the secondary

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:05.700
predation spike. Today, many large-scale programs come by an aerial 1080 with ground control

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.840
of mustolids. This reflects an expanded view of the predator guild rather than a single

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:16.300
target approach. The original goal of lowering tuberculosis and protecting canopy trees

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:21.440
has continued. It is now pursued with greater attention to the indirect pathways created

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:27.100
by removing one abundant introduced species. In practice, this has meant scheduling additional

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:32.040
trap lines or using different toxins timed to the post-1080 window.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.960
The adjustment lengthens planning cycles, but reduces the period during which Kiwi

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.160
nests remain exposed.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:43.860
When a New Zealand agency's measured success solely by possum density and cattle reactor

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.800
rates, the program rewarded rapid reduction of that single population, even while it temporarily

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.960
increased predation pressure on Kiwi.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:58.880
Later, operations that added stote control after each 1080 drop show how the same landscape

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.860
can be managed once the food web link is included in the planning metric.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.920
Managers who now sequence or combine treatments for both possums and stotes illustrate how an

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:14.200
initial focus on one introduced mammal can be widened without abandoning the original public

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.960
health and forest protection aims.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:21.280
How might current single species eradication targets elsewhere account for the predators

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.960
or pray that will respond once the first population falls.

